WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.544 --> 00:00:02.982
- We had breakdowns in the system.

00:00:02.982 --> 00:00:05.612
We ended up on the front page
of the Wall Street Journal.

00:00:05.612 --> 00:00:07.844
Find every excuse to have a party.

00:00:07.844 --> 00:00:09.457
So I think innovation is everywhere.

00:00:09.457 --> 00:00:12.326
I think it's in the structure
of your organization.

00:00:12.326 --> 00:00:13.923
You've got to be over here with the needle

00:00:13.923 --> 00:00:15.181
to get the needle here.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:18.132
Somebody's out there waiting to pluck you.

00:00:18.132 --> 00:00:20.902
Or anyone in the world
and say, "I'm dreaming.

00:00:20.902 --> 00:00:23.239
"Please, I'm not going to
make any numbers for a while."

00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:25.466
People today are just better.

00:00:25.466 --> 00:00:29.605
You grow from the reflected
glory of your people.

00:00:29.605 --> 00:00:31.651
Never do what the assignment is.

00:00:31.651 --> 00:00:32.757
Do more than that.

00:00:32.757 --> 00:00:35.097
Thousands of people are walking around

00:00:35.097 --> 00:00:37.411
frightened to death about
what's going to happen.

00:00:37.411 --> 00:00:39.598
- He's retired American
business executive,

00:00:39.598 --> 00:00:41.520
author and chemical engineer.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.528
- He was chairman and
CEO of General Electric.

00:00:44.528 --> 00:00:46.680
- Between 1981 and 2001.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.485
- During his tenure at GE,

00:00:48.485 --> 00:00:52.056
the company's value rose 4000%.

00:00:52.056 --> 00:00:56.223
- He's Jack Welch, and here's
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:58.766 --> 00:01:02.604
- Well, I was tumultuous
in the early career.

00:01:02.604 --> 00:01:04.604
I was totally impetuous.

00:01:06.245 --> 00:01:07.144
I've always had a stamina,

00:01:07.144 --> 00:01:09.508
so that's a weakness from
the beginning to the end,

00:01:09.508 --> 00:01:11.871
I think that'll be there.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:12.954
I think if I,

00:01:18.627 --> 00:01:20.450
I made mistakes on deals.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.023
I'd openly admit it.

00:01:22.023 --> 00:01:22.901
I'd walk around talking about it.

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:25.434
If I can make this big a dopey move,

00:01:25.434 --> 00:01:26.440
anybody can make a move.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.149
Don't be paralyzed.

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:30.643
I made lots of mistakes on people.

00:01:30.643 --> 00:01:32.433
I hired people, in the early days,

00:01:32.433 --> 00:01:36.178
I hired almost always on
resumes and slickness,

00:01:36.178 --> 00:01:39.296
which is probably inversely
proportional to talent.

00:01:39.296 --> 00:01:42.346
(audience laughs)

00:01:42.346 --> 00:01:45.317
So I did a number of these things

00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:46.534
on a fairly frequent basis.

00:01:46.534 --> 00:01:48.464
But I talked about it.

00:01:48.464 --> 00:01:51.381
I think the thing is, talking about

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:57.195
what your mistakes are helps
an organization enormously.

00:01:57.195 --> 00:01:59.400
It allows them to feel free to make them.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.307
I mean, I bought an investment banking.

00:02:02.307 --> 00:02:04.928
No one could make a
mistake bigger than that.

00:02:04.928 --> 00:02:07.187
Boo boos that we had, we
didn't have to run ads,

00:02:07.187 --> 00:02:09.411
we had breakdowns in the system,

00:02:09.411 --> 00:02:10.990
we ended up on the front page

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:15.073
of the Wall Street Journal
17 times in 14 months,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.077
the right hand column, being called dopes.

00:02:20.077 --> 00:02:21.469
That's not fun.

00:02:21.469 --> 00:02:23.521
And we went through that because

00:02:23.521 --> 00:02:26.591
it was a cultural error
that I personally made.

00:02:26.591 --> 00:02:30.159
And I owned up to it, talked
to everybody about it.

00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:32.576
A boss's entire job is to get

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:37.399
the combined intellect of his team

00:02:37.399 --> 00:02:39.614
to be raised to a new level,

00:02:39.614 --> 00:02:40.978
and to instill spirit,

00:02:40.978 --> 00:02:44.006
find every way to celebrate.

00:02:44.006 --> 00:02:47.242
Find every excuse to have a party.

00:02:47.242 --> 00:02:49.464
Every little victory counts.

00:02:49.464 --> 00:02:50.881
I mean, it is so,

00:02:52.554 --> 00:02:55.405
if a boss can't energize,

00:02:55.405 --> 00:03:00.044
if he or she can't excite and
make people want to be there,

00:03:00.044 --> 00:03:02.005
they shouldn't have that title.

00:03:02.005 --> 00:03:03.271
Well, I think people too often

00:03:03.271 --> 00:03:07.146
think of innovation as going to the lab,

00:03:07.146 --> 00:03:09.648
going off in a garage and
designing something new

00:03:09.648 --> 00:03:11.219
and bringing it to market.

00:03:11.219 --> 00:03:12.994
I think innovation's all around you.

00:03:12.994 --> 00:03:16.959
You see or smell something
that somebody else is doing,

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:20.531
you adapt it to your place
and take it to new levels,

00:03:20.531 --> 00:03:22.738
that's a breakthrough for your company.

00:03:22.738 --> 00:03:25.193
And the process never stops.

00:03:25.193 --> 00:03:28.511
And if you've got a company
that has a mentality inside

00:03:28.511 --> 00:03:32.562
that is filled with searching
for a better idea every day,

00:03:32.562 --> 00:03:36.667
not just as a slogan
but as a real concept,

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:39.571
you will have innovation
around you all the time.

00:03:39.571 --> 00:03:40.904
Classic example,

00:03:41.976 --> 00:03:43.934
we had an innovation by going to India.

00:03:43.934 --> 00:03:46.875
We went to India in the late 80s.

00:03:46.875 --> 00:03:49.023
And we went there to sell lots of products

00:03:49.023 --> 00:03:51.267
to a billion people.

00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:54.048
Well, 15 years later, we still haven't

00:03:54.048 --> 00:03:56.487
reached a billion dollars in sales.

00:03:56.487 --> 00:03:59.228
But we've got 41,000 employees

00:03:59.228 --> 00:04:01.769
because we found intellect.

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:03.120
And we were the first people to go there

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.491
and build huge R and D
center, technology centers,

00:04:07.491 --> 00:04:08.440
by using the intellect.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.432
That's innovation.

00:04:10.432 --> 00:04:13.237
Getting stuck in one
corner when bureaucracy

00:04:13.237 --> 00:04:14.854
won't let you sell products,

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:18.290
but finding another answer, intellect,

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:19.824
and capitalizing on it.

00:04:19.824 --> 00:04:21.428
So I think innovation is everywhere.

00:04:21.428 --> 00:04:24.613
I think it's in the structure
of your organization.

00:04:24.613 --> 00:04:27.663
I think it's in every
process you deal with.

00:04:27.663 --> 00:04:30.711
It's just finding a bit better
way to do things every day.

00:04:30.711 --> 00:04:32.761
And I think everything
has to be a closed loop.

00:04:32.761 --> 00:04:35.767
You drive it with people, you
measure it with the people,

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:37.500
and then you go visit it.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:38.985
And you go visit the progress.

00:04:38.985 --> 00:04:41.610
Now, you'll get a lot of air

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.149
and nonsense when you go visit.

00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:47.639
For example, in Six Sigma,
if the amount of savings

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:51.117
I was presented in the course of Six Sigma

00:04:51.117 --> 00:04:52.950
were all added up, it
would have been larger

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.175
than the GDP of the United States.

00:04:55.175 --> 00:04:56.148
(audience laughs)

00:04:56.148 --> 00:04:59.315
I mean, the PowerPoint slides

00:04:59.315 --> 00:05:01.815
can accumulate enormous depth.

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:05.658
So all these things
are hyped to an extend.

00:05:05.658 --> 00:05:07.158
But they got to be.

00:05:09.192 --> 00:05:13.025
You cannot make change
with rational behavior.

00:05:15.357 --> 00:05:17.514
You cannot make change by,

00:05:17.514 --> 00:05:19.860
"let's have quality program.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.790
"Quality is good, everybody.

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:23.246
"Let's go at it.

00:05:23.246 --> 00:05:26.200
"Let's make sure our customers
feel good with our quality."

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.667
You got to say, "no, our quality stinks.

00:05:29.667 --> 00:05:31.167
"We've got to improve it dramatically."

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:32.749
Only the best people can be on it.

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:35.596
Half of our offices by the year 2005

00:05:35.596 --> 00:05:37.902
will have been black belts in quality.

00:05:37.902 --> 00:05:39.379
The stock options and the awards

00:05:39.379 --> 00:05:41.505
are going to those leading the efforts.

00:05:41.505 --> 00:05:44.155
And you've got to be a maniac about it.

00:05:44.155 --> 00:05:47.050
To move it, you've got to
be over here with the needle

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:48.782
to gt the needle here.

00:05:48.782 --> 00:05:51.801
If you are sitting there,
comfortable in what you're doing,

00:05:51.801 --> 00:05:53.431
you can be sure,

00:05:53.431 --> 00:05:56.284
with your nice awards of number one here,

00:05:56.284 --> 00:05:59.909
number one here, number one Zagat,

00:05:59.909 --> 00:06:03.720
and number one this one,
number one that one,

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.259
somebody's out there waiting to pluck you.

00:06:07.259 --> 00:06:09.759
And you cant' get comfortable.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.226
And at that rate of change,
if you start seeing stuff,

00:06:13.226 --> 00:06:15.183
you've got to move.

00:06:15.183 --> 00:06:16.889
The only job of a leader is

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:19.639
when you ran for Europe or anyone

00:06:20.674 --> 00:06:22.720
in this room running businesses,

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.578
the only reason for having the job

00:06:24.578 --> 00:06:26.904
is to make the right trade-offs,

00:06:26.904 --> 00:06:29.659
between cutting costs, productivity,

00:06:29.659 --> 00:06:33.826
efficiency and developing
new ideas and new products.

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:36.803
Anybody in the world, for example,

00:06:36.803 --> 00:06:38.068
I love the EC.

00:06:38.068 --> 00:06:38.901
He was always going to say,

00:06:38.901 --> 00:06:42.942
"Oh, I hate these analysts presentations.

00:06:42.942 --> 00:06:45.791
"Analysts are pressing me
for quarterly earnings,"

00:06:45.791 --> 00:06:47.397
et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:47.397 --> 00:06:52.005
Look, any jerk in the
world can squeeze all day

00:06:52.005 --> 00:06:55.771
and say, "talk to me in
five years about my growth."

00:06:55.771 --> 00:06:58.504
Or anyone in the world
can say, "I'm dreaming.

00:06:58.504 --> 00:07:01.325
"Please, I'm not going to
make any numbers for a while."

00:07:01.325 --> 00:07:03.253
You can squeeze or you can dream.

00:07:03.253 --> 00:07:04.765
But a manger does both.

00:07:04.765 --> 00:07:06.265
A leader does both.

00:07:06.265 --> 00:07:07.802
You dream and you squeeze.

00:07:07.802 --> 00:07:09.802
You eat while you dream.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.440
And there's no getting around it.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.409
That's why you're paid.

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:18.167
Any jerk can do one or the other.

00:07:18.167 --> 00:07:19.708
(audience laughs)

00:07:19.708 --> 00:07:22.298
But it's your job to
make those trade offs.

00:07:22.298 --> 00:07:26.001
You've got to see everyone as a mentor.

00:07:26.001 --> 00:07:26.875
Your whole crowd,

00:07:26.875 --> 00:07:28.851
somebody who works for
you might be a mentor.

00:07:28.851 --> 00:07:30.745
I remember when I became CEO,

00:07:30.745 --> 00:07:34.040
I was not a great financial wizard.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:35.588
I never did become a financial wizard.

00:07:35.588 --> 00:07:39.290
But at least I had this
fellow who worked for me,

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:43.457
10 years my junior, 33
years when we made him CFO.

00:07:45.022 --> 00:07:47.618
He basically dumped his brain into mine.

00:07:47.618 --> 00:07:50.035
I live with him, as we
went through all this,

00:07:50.035 --> 00:07:52.005
left side, right side, et cetera,

00:07:52.005 --> 00:07:53.841
the balance sheet from A to Z,

00:07:53.841 --> 00:07:57.526
and he was an enormously valuable person.

00:07:57.526 --> 00:08:00.026
I've had people who were on my board

00:08:00.026 --> 00:08:03.693
who were completely
committed to our success

00:08:04.921 --> 00:08:07.367
who would challenge every
people appointment I would make,

00:08:07.367 --> 00:08:09.934
who would ask me, "are you sure?"

00:08:09.934 --> 00:08:12.487
I think the business
media is a great mentor.

00:08:12.487 --> 00:08:13.320
You may laugh at that,

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.704
but I think Fortune, Business Week,

00:08:15.704 --> 00:08:17.871
there's a business language.

00:08:17.871 --> 00:08:20.816
I find the mentors read every story.

00:08:20.816 --> 00:08:24.241
I can't understand why
somebody can be in business

00:08:24.241 --> 00:08:25.907
and not read all those stories.

00:08:25.907 --> 00:08:28.722
I think you'll learn what
strategies didn't work,

00:08:28.722 --> 00:08:31.661
did work, some of the stories
aren't quite accurate,

00:08:31.661 --> 00:08:33.526
some of them aren't true, so what?

00:08:33.526 --> 00:08:35.646
You're constantly learning
the rhythm of business.

00:08:35.646 --> 00:08:39.313
So education and teaching
is all around you.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:44.724
As far as today, really,
we have an enormous cadre.

00:08:44.724 --> 00:08:48.506
People today are just better
than they were before.

00:08:48.506 --> 00:08:50.230
It's more competitive.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:52.610
It's no longer, when you become CEO today,

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:54.568
it's no longer the coronation.

00:08:54.568 --> 00:08:57.927
It's the beginning of a
new competitive career.

00:08:57.927 --> 00:09:01.379
I remember in 1980 when I became CEO,

00:09:01.379 --> 00:09:04.623
I would go to these meetings
where people would sit around,

00:09:04.623 --> 00:09:07.716
and their briefing books
prepared by huge staff,

00:09:07.716 --> 00:09:10.788
and they pontificated about
the economy and this other.

00:09:10.788 --> 00:09:11.734
Nonsense stuff.

00:09:11.734 --> 00:09:12.685
It meant nothing.

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:14.314
They're just mouthing words.

00:09:14.314 --> 00:09:15.814
It was ridiculous.

00:09:16.676 --> 00:09:18.747
Now, today, people are in short sleeves,

00:09:18.747 --> 00:09:20.570
getting in there and doing it

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:23.082
because the global
competition is so tough.

00:09:23.082 --> 00:09:25.583
I think building an
organization, building a team,

00:09:25.583 --> 00:09:28.979
the idea that Jack Welch is the hero

00:09:28.979 --> 00:09:31.557
of creating all this value is silly.

00:09:31.557 --> 00:09:34.224
Getting great people in all these jobs,

00:09:34.224 --> 00:09:36.997
it's the team that
makes this thing happen.

00:09:36.997 --> 00:09:40.985
And so building a team
is your strongest suit.

00:09:40.985 --> 00:09:42.526
And you've got to love your team.

00:09:42.526 --> 00:09:44.351
You've got to want your
team to be promoted.

00:09:44.351 --> 00:09:45.647
And when you're coming up,

00:09:45.647 --> 00:09:49.368
the biggest problem I
find with the young MBAs,

00:09:49.368 --> 00:09:52.336
at first, the job is you shining,

00:09:52.336 --> 00:09:54.712
raising your hand, having the answers.

00:09:54.712 --> 00:09:56.068
They want to do that all the time.

00:09:56.068 --> 00:09:56.904
They just got out of school.

00:09:56.904 --> 00:09:59.268
They want to show you how smart they are.

00:09:59.268 --> 00:10:03.967
Now, the transition to
manager, it all changes.

00:10:03.967 --> 00:10:06.416
It's about growing your people, not you.

00:10:06.416 --> 00:10:10.499
You grow from the reflected
glory of your people.

00:10:11.526 --> 00:10:14.796
So it's all about making them flourish.

00:10:14.796 --> 00:10:17.575
I like to use the line that your job

00:10:17.575 --> 00:10:20.373
is walking around with a
can of water in one hand,

00:10:20.373 --> 00:10:22.533
a can of fertilizer in the other hand,

00:10:22.533 --> 00:10:26.052
sprinkling it on the seeds
and watching them grow.

00:10:26.052 --> 00:10:30.790
You'll get some weeds, and you
got to pull those weeds out.

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:32.789
So everyone's not going to flourish.

00:10:32.789 --> 00:10:37.151
But you've got to be
thinking about all the time,

00:10:37.151 --> 00:10:40.059
loving to see your people thrive.

00:10:40.059 --> 00:10:43.176
Loving to see them get promoted.

00:10:43.176 --> 00:10:44.393
It's a real turn on.

00:10:44.393 --> 00:10:46.996
It is what management's all about.

00:10:46.996 --> 00:10:49.922
It's about bringing to people

00:10:49.922 --> 00:10:52.172
great lives, great futures,

00:10:53.721 --> 00:10:55.319
changing their family's life.

00:10:55.319 --> 00:10:57.035
That's a turn on.

00:10:57.035 --> 00:10:57.962
It's a big deal.

00:10:57.962 --> 00:10:59.547
That's what you've got to think about.

00:10:59.547 --> 00:11:01.300
When you work in a business

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:03.708
and you're asked to do something,

00:11:03.708 --> 00:11:07.168
if you just do what the
boss asks you to do,

00:11:07.168 --> 00:11:09.751
you will end up just confirming

00:11:11.045 --> 00:11:13.862
what the boss wanted to have written down.

00:11:13.862 --> 00:11:17.290
They already knew the answer
when they asked the question.

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:20.051
So your job in the new
job is to over deliver.

00:11:20.051 --> 00:11:21.942
Give them new perspectives.

00:11:21.942 --> 00:11:22.951
When anybody asks you,

00:11:22.951 --> 00:11:25.250
schools ask you for a problem,

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:26.899
problem-solving and occassionally,

00:11:26.899 --> 00:11:28.596
then they ask you the questions.

00:11:28.596 --> 00:11:29.923
So you answer the questions.

00:11:29.923 --> 00:11:32.710
You don't get the questions in business.

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:35.991
You have to develop a
perspective that's wider.

00:11:35.991 --> 00:11:38.664
And I always tell the
story of my first job.

00:11:38.664 --> 00:11:41.512
I was a project manager in
a little plastic operation

00:11:41.512 --> 00:11:43.005
in Pittsfield, Mass,

00:11:43.005 --> 00:11:44.595
and my boss wrote, from New York,

00:11:44.595 --> 00:11:46.199
he was coming up to see,

00:11:46.199 --> 00:11:48.527
not my boss, my boss's boss.

00:11:48.527 --> 00:11:49.863
And they wanted me to come in

00:11:49.863 --> 00:11:52.628
and wear one those white shirt pictures

00:11:52.628 --> 00:11:56.046
to present the cases for my new plastic.

00:11:56.046 --> 00:11:57.338
Tell him where I was going.

00:11:57.338 --> 00:11:59.041
So I got all prepared.

00:11:59.041 --> 00:12:03.172
But I did do is, I didn't
talk about my project alone.

00:12:03.172 --> 00:12:07.089
I talked about the 10
plastics in the industry,

00:12:07.992 --> 00:12:09.490
where they were going to be in five years,

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.622
their cost structure, from the DOW,

00:12:11.622 --> 00:12:13.674
Silanes, Dupont, et cetera.

00:12:13.674 --> 00:12:15.011
I developed that package.

00:12:15.011 --> 00:12:19.506
And I gave my boss's boss
this picture of the industry.

00:12:19.506 --> 00:12:21.345
I think that's how I got noticed there.

00:12:21.345 --> 00:12:24.561
I got out of the pile because
I didn't just give him

00:12:24.561 --> 00:12:27.041
my project report, here's
what I did yesterday,

00:12:27.041 --> 00:12:27.874
here's what I'll do tomorrow,

00:12:27.874 --> 00:12:29.098
here's what we'll do next day.

00:12:29.098 --> 00:12:32.772
I gave him perspective,
and made him look smarter.

00:12:32.772 --> 00:12:36.059
He now knew more about the
industry than he ever knew.

00:12:36.059 --> 00:12:37.269
And that's what you want to do.

00:12:37.269 --> 00:12:40.075
You always want to over deliver.

00:12:40.075 --> 00:12:41.992
Never do what the assignment is.

00:12:41.992 --> 00:12:43.379
Do more than that.

00:12:43.379 --> 00:12:46.789
Wider, new insights, new horizons.

00:12:46.789 --> 00:12:48.775
Well, if you're a leader,

00:12:48.775 --> 00:12:50.704
you clearly have to demonstrate it.

00:12:50.704 --> 00:12:52.399
You have to be open.

00:12:52.399 --> 00:12:54.356
You have to be able to admit mistakes.

00:12:54.356 --> 00:12:57.863
You have to be able to cut
through the business-ese.

00:12:57.863 --> 00:13:00.884
You've got to be able
to talk to every person

00:13:00.884 --> 00:13:02.474
in your organization in such a way

00:13:02.474 --> 00:13:04.249
that they know where they stand.

00:13:04.249 --> 00:13:06.023
No one can be a manager without,

00:13:06.023 --> 00:13:09.440
no one can call themselves a manager

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:10.819
and have employees working for them

00:13:10.819 --> 00:13:14.469
when the employees don't know
exactly where they stand,

00:13:14.469 --> 00:13:15.986
where they are relative to others,

00:13:15.986 --> 00:13:19.057
and where they're going in their career.

00:13:19.057 --> 00:13:21.390
When you're debating a deal,

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:24.934
you've got to have an atmosphere

00:13:24.934 --> 00:13:27.440
where everybody gets it on the table.

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:29.941
Not massive PowerPoint slides,

00:13:29.941 --> 00:13:32.804
numbing chats that put people to sleep.

00:13:32.804 --> 00:13:34.888
But really dialogue, face-to-face,

00:13:34.888 --> 00:13:36.832
eyeball-to-eyeball confrontation

00:13:36.832 --> 00:13:39.686
about why the deal works,
why it doesn't work.

00:13:39.686 --> 00:13:43.171
And you demonstrate that yourself.

00:13:43.171 --> 00:13:44.710
You, yourself, would be,

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:48.043
if the place is tied up in its knickers,

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:51.804
it'll be because you are
setting an atmosphere

00:13:51.804 --> 00:13:54.054
that isn't open, isn't fun.

00:13:56.152 --> 00:13:58.265
We used to make fun of
people that wouldn't be open.

00:13:58.265 --> 00:14:01.267
You know, we'd josh about it,

00:14:01.267 --> 00:14:05.434
just to try and lighten the
thing up so they'd get there.

00:14:06.701 --> 00:14:07.894
Candor can kill you, though,

00:14:07.894 --> 00:14:11.420
because candor, if you're
in a stiff, rigid hierarchy,

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.326
you've got to be able to use it right.

00:14:13.326 --> 00:14:15.882
Because if nobody above
you is practicing it,

00:14:15.882 --> 00:14:18.175
and you come in swinging from the fences,

00:14:18.175 --> 00:14:20.175
it could be troublesome.

00:14:21.642 --> 00:14:24.457
I happen to believe it speeds
everything in business.

00:14:24.457 --> 00:14:26.497
It speeds decision-making,

00:14:26.497 --> 00:14:29.577
it moves the organization
competitively faster,

00:14:29.577 --> 00:14:33.494
it gets people to be open
about where they fit.

00:14:36.182 --> 00:14:38.764
I was over at the BBC this week,

00:14:38.764 --> 00:14:41.333
and this place is the most paralyzed place

00:14:41.333 --> 00:14:42.603
I've seen in my lifetime.

00:14:42.603 --> 00:14:44.865
(audience laughs)

00:14:44.865 --> 00:14:47.190
Thousands of people are walking around

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:49.177
fighting to death about
what's going to happen.

00:14:49.177 --> 00:14:50.135
Are they going to get sacked.

00:14:50.135 --> 00:14:52.800
Every interview I had, they asked about,

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:55.535
"well, somebody here is
going to get sacked,"

00:14:55.535 --> 00:14:57.019
while we're going through here.

00:14:57.019 --> 00:15:00.088
And no one knew where they stood.

00:15:00.088 --> 00:15:03.339
And so the management
there is just being cruel.

00:15:03.339 --> 00:15:05.005
So employees are walking around

00:15:05.005 --> 00:15:07.015
not knowing where they stand.

00:15:07.015 --> 00:15:08.840
You can't have an organization

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:10.426
where people don't know,

00:15:10.426 --> 00:15:12.535
you, as a professor of
organizational dynamics,

00:15:12.535 --> 00:15:13.836
you know that you've got

00:15:13.836 --> 00:15:15.127
to let people know where they stand.

00:15:15.127 --> 00:15:18.639
They can't be in this
fight and paralysis state.

00:15:18.639 --> 00:15:20.084
Nothing happens.

00:15:20.084 --> 00:15:21.271
- Thanks so much for watching.

00:15:21.271 --> 00:15:24.568
- We meet these people because
Ryan J Davis asked us to.

00:15:24.568 --> 00:15:26.100
- So if you have a famous entrepreneur

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.094
you want us to profile next--

00:15:28.094 --> 00:15:29.666
- Leave it down in the
comments section below,

00:15:29.666 --> 00:15:31.123
and we'll see what we can do.

00:15:31.123 --> 00:15:33.265
- Also, we love to know which one

00:15:33.265 --> 00:15:36.224
of Jack Welch's top 10
rules meant the most to you.

00:15:36.224 --> 00:15:37.573
- Leave it in the comments below,

00:15:37.573 --> 00:15:39.164
and we'll join in the discussion.

00:15:39.164 --> 00:15:40.853
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:15:40.853 --> 00:15:44.028
Continue to believe,
and we'll see you soon.

00:15:44.028 --> 00:15:47.195
(thunder sound booms)

00:15:49.835 --> 00:15:52.498
- Differentiation is a big value of mine.

00:15:52.498 --> 00:15:54.916
I don't believe you can run a company

00:15:54.916 --> 00:15:58.681
without having an organization
that differentiates,

00:15:58.681 --> 00:16:02.606
differentiates on how it
treats different businesses,

00:16:02.606 --> 00:16:04.599
some are not equal,

00:16:04.599 --> 00:16:06.697
and people, not equal.

00:16:06.697 --> 00:16:10.224
Takes care of the stars,
motivates the middle,

00:16:10.224 --> 00:16:13.191
and asks the bottom to move on.

00:16:13.191 --> 00:16:14.899
I believe that.

00:16:14.899 --> 00:16:18.035
You think the Patriots
keep the worst players?

00:16:18.035 --> 00:16:20.992
Why should a sports team
keep the worst players?

00:16:20.992 --> 00:16:22.862
The silliest thing that I hear

00:16:22.862 --> 00:16:24.411
when we talk about differentiation,

00:16:24.411 --> 00:16:26.306
and we get this all the time is,

00:16:26.306 --> 00:16:29.293
how can you possibly be telling somebody

00:16:29.293 --> 00:16:31.915
they're not that good and
somebody else is that good?

00:16:31.915 --> 00:16:33.969
Well, why should you stop getting grades

00:16:33.969 --> 00:16:35.969
the day you leave the U?

00:16:37.111 --> 00:16:40.056
Why should a grown-up
not be able to grade,

00:16:40.056 --> 00:16:43.696
but a sixth grader is
given best in the class,

00:16:43.696 --> 00:16:44.936
and weakest in the class?

00:16:44.936 --> 00:16:48.240
It's the dumbest idea in the world.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:50.626
So you want to tell
people where they stand.

00:16:50.626 --> 00:16:53.679
As a manager, people always
have to know where they stand.

00:16:53.679 --> 00:16:55.496
You can't be turning away from it

00:16:55.496 --> 00:16:58.823
and then one day going in and
saying we're having a layoff.

00:16:58.823 --> 00:17:02.617
Now, when Ford has this big 30,000 layoff,

00:17:02.617 --> 00:17:05.148
hopefully they got a
performance appraisal system

00:17:05.148 --> 00:17:07.453
that somebody won't be saying, "why me?"

00:17:07.453 --> 00:17:08.857
And somebody says, "well,
you weren't very good."

00:17:08.857 --> 00:17:10.286
And they pull out the appraisals

00:17:10.286 --> 00:17:13.691
that say they were great
for the last 10 years.

00:17:13.691 --> 00:17:17.192
You want candor, you want differentiation,

00:17:17.192 --> 00:17:18.835
and those are things I believe in.

00:17:18.835 --> 00:17:21.875
I walked around GE preaching constantly.

00:17:21.875 --> 00:17:24.958
Don't you ever think you're a victim.

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:29.294
If you ever feel like a victim, get out.

00:17:29.294 --> 00:17:30.460
Because if enough of you get out,

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:33.266
we'll find out who's making
you feel like a victim.

00:17:33.266 --> 00:17:35.846
It's your job to take responsibility.

00:17:35.846 --> 00:17:37.649
Go find another game.

00:17:37.649 --> 00:17:38.944
There's plenty of games here.

00:17:38.944 --> 00:17:40.550
Unemployment's low here.

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:42.580
Go find something else.

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:43.608
Go on your own.

00:17:43.608 --> 00:17:44.492
Do something.

00:17:44.492 --> 00:17:47.754
But, Jesus, don't spend
most of your waking hours

00:17:47.754 --> 00:17:49.156
feeling like a victim.

00:17:49.156 --> 00:17:52.789
If you ever say some thing's
important in your organization,

00:17:52.789 --> 00:17:55.294
you better put your best person on it.

00:17:55.294 --> 00:17:56.921
Because your whole organization

00:17:56.921 --> 00:17:58.899
is evaluating how important it is

00:17:58.899 --> 00:18:00.720
by who you staff it with.

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:02.586
The biggest mistake CEOs make is,

00:18:02.586 --> 00:18:04.619
"let me give you this big puffy speech

00:18:04.619 --> 00:18:06.586
"about how vertical this is.

00:18:06.586 --> 00:18:08.705
"Inventory terms are critical.

00:18:08.705 --> 00:18:11.230
"We need to get our cash flow improved."

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:14.316
And then they hire Joe four
months from retirement,

00:18:14.316 --> 00:18:15.569
to lead the effort.

00:18:15.569 --> 00:18:18.586
And the whole place just goes into a yawn.

00:18:18.586 --> 00:18:19.895
Joe is like a gas pain.

00:18:19.895 --> 00:18:21.062
He'll go away.

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:25.917
And that's what happens to the initiative.

00:18:25.917 --> 00:18:28.732
And so it's an enormous problem.

00:18:28.732 --> 00:18:32.916
You say something's
important, staff it that way.

00:18:32.916 --> 00:18:34.112
Follow up that way.

00:18:34.112 --> 00:18:37.191
Close loop measure that way.

00:18:37.191 --> 00:18:39.586
But I'm sure many of
you have had experiences

00:18:39.586 --> 00:18:41.390
where people would articulate

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:43.867
the criticalness of an issue

00:18:43.867 --> 00:18:47.152
and then put some okay player on it.

00:18:47.152 --> 00:18:48.323
Only your best.

